counterfactuals.output:
A computer-implemented method for optimizing portioning of a workpiece, the method comprising:

1. receiving workpiece data representative of at least one geometric characteristic of the workpiece;

2. establishing, relative to a coordinate system associated with the workpiece, a plurality of reference coordinates defining candidate locations for one or more cuts;

3. generating a simulated portioning operation by applying the one or more cuts to a digital representation of the workpiece at the candidate locations;

4. determining, from the simulated portioning operation, predicted characteristics of a plurality of resulting final pieces, the predicted characteristics comprising at least one of a dimension, shape, volume, mass, yield, quality classification, or deviation from a target specification;

5. comparing the predicted characteristics with one or more desired final-piece characteristics;

6. responsive to determining that the predicted characteristics fail to satisfy at least one acceptance criterion associated with the desired final-piece characteristics, editing at least one of the reference coordinates to define a revised candidate location for at least one cut;

7. repeating the generating, determining, comparing, and editing operations using the revised candidate location until the predicted characteristics satisfy the at least one acceptance criterion or until a predetermined termination condition is met; and

8. outputting a portioning specification comprising final reference coordinates for controlling or guiding a cutting apparatus to portion the workpiece.

In certain embodiments, editing the reference coordinates comprises translating, rotating, adding, deleting, or reordering one or more cut references. An objective function may be applied during each iteration to minimize waste, dimensional deviation, processing time, or a weighted combination thereof, while satisfying constraints relating to equipment capability, minimum final-piece dimensions, cut width, defect location, material orientation, or product quality. The final reference coordinates may be transmitted directly to a controller of the cutting apparatus or presented to an operator for approval prior to execution.
